              "label": "Figure as canvas, Axes as plotting area — the architecture explained",
              "summary": "The presenter defines the three-level hierarchy: a Figure is the top-level container — the entire window or page — while an Axes (spelled with an 'e') is the actual plot area where a specific piece of data is drawn, and a single Figure can hold multiple Axes. The axis (no 'e') is the familiar x- or y-axis that lives inside one Axes. The segment closes by restating the canvas framing: Figure is the canvas, each Axes is one painting on it.",
              "fact_ids": [
